we start today's program in turkey where  a lot of people are currently facing a  difficult time because of surging prices  the new year begins with long queues for  bread which is at least still affordable  fruit meat and fish have become luxury  goods for many turks  vendors are stuck with their perishable  products their customers are forced to  budget their liras tightly the currency  is rapidly losing its value it's a cause  of anger for many in turkey  and most of that anger is directed at  him turkish president recep tayyip  erdogan many blame him for the drastic  plunge of the lira they are worried that  erdogan's economic policies will be the  financial ruin of many turks the  inflation is particularly hard on the  country's poorest  people like embracer he's a waste  material collector even before the  current crisis it was difficult for him  to make ends meet and now he barely  earns enough to feed his young family  an old cardboard box means hard cash for  emra hazir  the 25 year old earns money by  collecting waste paper from garbage cans  in istanbul a metropolis with a  population of over 15 million he also  collects plastic  it's hard to make a living because in  times of economic crisis the prices of  recyclable materials are plummeting  a kilo of cardboard gets you the  equivalent of three euro cents a kilo of  plastic only 10 cents instead of 12  cents  every day emra spends 10 hours wandering  the streets of istanbul in search of  paper and plastic he's just one of  thousands of collectors of recyclable  materials struggling to survive in  turkey  the price of bread has gone up again  even the price for the cheapest bread  has doubled  how can i afford enough food  but i have to keep working  a derelict building on the outskirts of  the city serves as a collection point  where emra delivers his findings at the  end of the day  he collected 58 kilos today and earned  the equivalent of less than 5 euros  after sorting the garbage is later  transported to recycling plants  the streets would be full of litter  we prevent the garbage cans from  overflowing  that was true up until now but in the  midst of the economic crisis of all  times turkish authorities plan to let  private companies collect recyclables  it's a lucrative market as a result the  mostly illegal collection points will  disappear  a few months ago an eviction order led  to violent clashes between waste  collectors and the police  there were dozens of arrests  meanwhile the recyclables workers have  joined forces to fight back against the  allegations  they say that we employ illegal refugees  we pose a security problem in  residential neighborhoods  and we disturb the peace of local  residents  in a press statement  the governor of istanbul  called on citizens to work together to  ensure that the carts of waste  collectors disappear from the streets  amra has to share his living space in  istanbul with three other collectors  they all come from the far east of  turkey  he only sees his wife and one-year-old  child every three months  sometimes i sit here and i wish i could  be home with them  but i can't  my mother just called the other day  and said they don't have money for  diapers so i know i have to keep going  here  the next morning emra sends clothes and  toys home to his family with a relative  he found the toys in the trash  but it's not only the country's  estimated 500 000 garbage collectors who  are currently fearing for their  livelihoods  the value of the turkish lira has almost  halved in the past year and inflation is  officially at 36 percent  economic experts say it's actually much  higher  in surveys more than half of the  population say they can barely afford  their daily groceries  almost three quarters of turks are in  debt  and almost everyone complains about a  drop in income  these figures show how desperate the  situation is  at the collection point operations  continue but for how much longer  emra and his colleagues hope the  government will hold off in changing the  recycling system for a while longer  collecting and sorting recyclables is a  very complex matter  and difficult to organize  after all  there is no waste separation in turkish  households  so for now emra is still needed on the  streets of istanbul but every day the  economic situation worsens  and for emra that means he must collect  even more garbage to make ends meet  you
 

